<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Billy Hughes</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1862">1862</a>–<a href="/years/1952">1952</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as William Morris Hughes</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Billy Hughes was prime minister of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a> from 1915 to 1923 and led the country through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>. His campaign for conscription, rejected in referendums in 1916 and 1917, split the Labor Party and led him to form the Nationalist Party. At the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris-peace-conference-1919-1920">Paris Peace Conference</a> he secured a mandate over German <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-guinea">New Guinea</a> and opposed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>'s racial equality proposal.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/billy-hughes.jpg" alt="Billy Hughes" width="320" height="448" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Fairfax Corporation · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Billy_Hughes_1919.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>William Morris Hughes was born on 25 September 1862 in Pimlico, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, to Welsh parents: a carpenter and joiner at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palace-of-westminster">Palace of Westminster</a> and a domestic servant. His mother died when he was six, and he was raised by relatives in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a>, mostly with an aunt who kept a boarding house in Llandudno. At eleven he entered St Stephen's School, Westminster, and later worked as a pupil-teacher.</p><p>At 22 he emigrated to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a> on an assisted passage, arriving in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brisbane">Brisbane</a> on 8 December 1884, and gave his birth year as 1864, a deception not uncovered until after his death. He worked at odd jobs, moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sydney-nova-scotia">Sydney</a> about 1886, and settled in Balmain, where he joined the Australian Socialist League. He was elected to the New South Wales Legislative Assembly in 1894.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>Hughes won the federal seat of West Sydney in 1901 and was called to the bar in 1903. He served as minister for external affairs in the Watson government of 1904 and as attorney-general in Andrew Fisher's governments, and became deputy leader of the Labor Party in 1907. When Fisher resigned in October 1915 with failing health, Hughes became prime minister. His support for sending conscripted troops overseas split the party, and he and his followers were expelled in November 1916. He stayed in office at the head of the National Labor Party, which soon merged with the Liberals to form the Nationalist Party, and his governments were re-elected in 1917 and 1919.</p><p>His government introduced a preferential voting system for the House of Representatives in 1918. In 1919 he went to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris-peace-conference-1919-1920">Paris Peace Conference</a> with Joseph Cook and signed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-versailles">Treaty of Versailles</a> for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a>, the first international treaty Australia had signed. He secured Australian control of the former German New Guinea. After the 1922 election the Nationalists needed a coalition with the Country Party, whose price was his resignation, and Stanley Bruce succeeded him in 1923.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Hughes remained in parliament for the rest of his life. In 1928 he and his supporters crossed the floor to bring down Bruce's government, and he later joined the United Australia Party, serving as a minister and warning against Japanese imperialism. He lost the 1939 leadership to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-menzies">Robert Menzies</a> by a handful of votes, and led the party briefly in 1941. He was expelled from the party in 1944 and joined the new <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liberal-party-uk">Liberal Party</a> in 1945.</p><p>He died on 28 October 1952, aged 90, at Lindfield, and some 450,000 people lined the streets for his state funeral in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sydney-nova-scotia">Sydney</a>. Troops called him the little digger. The labour movement considered him a traitor, and historians still debate his legacy.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Billy Hughes” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sydney">Sydney</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/australia">Australia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.51,-0.13&amp;year=1920" aria-label="London on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Billy Hughes"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4993 5724h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4993 5724h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=51.51,-0.13&amp;year=1920">What was here in 1920? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
